{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"teary-thistle-solitaire","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Observation\n`scripts/check_config_ownership.py:161` defines `derive_env_baseline()`, which calls\n`gen_env_registry.scan(REPO_ROOT / \"src\" / \"rebar\")` and unions in the env-channel\ndeprecation aliases. **It has zero callers anywhere in the repo** (verified:\n`grep -rn derive_env_baseline . --exclude-dir=.venv --exclude-dir=.git` returns only its own\ndefinition line).\n\n`check(root)` (`:562`) is `_validate_exceptions() + _check_registry_completeness() +\n_scan_files(root)` — it never routes through `derive_env_baseline`, so the ownership gate\nnever calls `scan()` at runtime.\n\n## Why it matters\n1. It is the **only** thing that made `scan()`'s 2-tuple arity load-bearing for this\n   consumer at runtime. That arity constraint is documented as load-bearing in\n   `gen_env_registry.scan`'s docstring and is repeated in ticket 84c7's acceptance\n   criteria. The constraint is still real for the module-level `import`, but the runtime\n   justification cited for it is a function nobody calls.\n2. It caused a **wrong inference during 84c7's investigation**: the plan reasoned that the\n   new `StaleEnvReadHelperRow` seam in `scan()` would transitively protect the ownership\n   gate. Measured: it does not. `check_config_ownership.py` exits 0 with a deliberately\n   stale row present; only `gen_env_registry.py --check` exits 1. The recurrence guard is\n   still portable and adequate (the drift gate plus the census test in `make test`), but\n   the coupling is not what the code reads as.\n3. Dead code in a CI gate script is a maintenance hazard: it is not exercised, so it can\n   silently rot (as the `KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS` rows themselves did).\n\n## Options (decide on evidence — do not assume)\n- **Delete it**, if nothing ever intended to call it. Check its introducing commit and\n  ticket first: it may be an intentionally-exported reuse surface for another consumer.\n- **Wire it in**, if `check()` was *meant* to validate its findings against the scanned env\n  baseline and the call was dropped. That would be a behaviour change to a CI gate and needs\n  its own plan review, not a drive-by.\n- **Keep and document** as an exported helper, with a test that exercises it.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] The introducing commit and ticket for `derive_env_baseline` are identified and\n      recorded, establishing whether the missing call is an omission or intentional.\n- [ ] Exactly one of delete / wire-in / document-and-test is applied, with the reasoning\n      recorded on this ticket.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck` and `make test` stay green.\n\n## Provenance\nFound while fixing bug 84c7 (surprised-convincing-gopher). Deliberately NOT folded into\nthat change: 84c7 is scoped to restoring the table->tree invariant, and wiring or deleting a\nfunction in a different CI gate would widen that diff past its proven mechanism.","id":"2a3e-e022-1f80-4c5f","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"derive_env_baseline() in check_config_ownership.py is dead code with zero callers"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787312050557276001,"uuid":"169b9aa7-f5cf-4e66-95c0-e3c3bfa4da7b"}